<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumo Xi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kumo Xi, or Xi, were a nomadic people of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a> region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, closely related to the Khitan and traced by Chinese sources to the Yuwen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>. They were subject in turn to the Turks, the Tang and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, and from the 10th century were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Khitan Liao</a> state, where they formed a distinct part of its army and administration.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> called them the Tatabi. Chinese histories place them among the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghu">Donghu</a> origin: the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> says the Xi were originally called the Kumo Xi, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-book-of-tang">New Book of Tang</a> says that after a defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> they took refuge in the Wuwan Mountains and that the leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadun">Tadun</a>, killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, was of their line. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-wei">Book of Wei</a> records that the Kumo Xi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> spoke the same language and fled together to the region of Songmo.</p><p>The meaning of the name is disputed. Omeljan Pritsak reconstructed it as qu(o)mâġ-ġay, the first part related to Mongolian and Turkic words for fine sand; Peter Benjamin Golden reads it as desert Qay or sand Qay, after their earlier homeland. Yury Zuev derived Tatabi from an Iranic phrase for falling waters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 388 the Kumo Xi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> fought the Northern Wei dynasty. The war weakened the Kumo Xi far more than the Khitans, and the two peoples split into separate polities. By the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, in about the 7th century, the Xi were subordinate to the Khitans, but after the Khitans' Li-Sun Rebellion of 696–697 and the revolt of Ketuyu in 730–734 they regained the upper hand.</p><p>A period of strength followed, from about 755 to 847. The Xi were friendly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a>, backed his rebellion and often raided Han territory. These raids drew heavy Tang responses, and battles in the 760s and in 795 went badly for them; after 795 they paid tribute to the Tang. When the Uyghur Khaganate collapsed in the 840s, the Xi rebelled in 847 and were crushed by Zhang Zhongwu, frontier commander of Lulong. They never recovered, and in the late ninth century the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> absorbed what remained of them, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> in 907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Xiqin, a bowed string instrument, is believed to derive from an instrument of the Xi. It is regarded as the ancestor of the Chinese erhu and of the Mongolian khuuchir and morin khuur. The history of the Kumo Xi is closely linked to that of the better-known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a>, who absorbed them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kumo Xi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Khitan and <b>Kumo Xi</b> are usually traced to the Yuwen Xianbei.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Kumo Xi</b> and Khitan were said to descend from Yuwen remnants.</blockquote><a href="/state/yuwen-xianbei">Yuwen Xianbei</a></li></ul></section>
